\section{Extended Downstream Evaluation}
\label{app:downstream}

Table~\ref{tab:downstream} compares Dense, KL 30\% (uniform allocation), and TEAL 30\% on three additional downstream benchmarks not included in the main results.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Extended downstream benchmarks at 30\% sparsity (LLaMA-3.1-8B, seed 42). HellaSwag: 10-shot normalized acc; PIQA: 0-shot normalized acc; BoolQ: 0-shot acc.}
\label{tab:downstream}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & HellaSwag & PIQA & BoolQ \\
\midrule
Dense & \textbf{82.33} & \textbf{80.74} & \textbf{82.94} \\
KL 30\% uniform & 72.70 & 75.95 & 76.85 \\ % exp_id: downstream_extended_kl30_s42
TEAL 30\% vanilla & 66.70 & 73.61 & 68.50 \\ % exp_id: downstream_teal30_baseline
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The KL predictor outperforms TEAL on all three benchmarks. On HellaSwag, it preserves 88.3\% of dense accuracy (72.70 vs.\ 82.33) vs.\ 81.0\% for TEAL (66.70). BoolQ shows the largest gap: 92.7\% vs.\ 82.6\% preservation. PIQA shows a smaller differential (94.1\% vs.\ 91.2\%).

The KL advantage ratio (computed within each architecture under matched allocation) is consistent across architectures (within 8\% at $\leq$50\% sparsity), with larger variance at 70\% (4.63$\times$ vs.\ 5.59$\times$) due to differential baseline collapse.
Consistent ratios despite Mistral's stronger TEAL baseline (gate distribution favors magnitude pruning) provide preliminary evidence of generality across SwiGLU architectures at the 7--8B scale.

\paragraph{Scale Generalization: Qwen-2.5-14B.}
On Qwen-2.5-14B~\cite{qwen2024qwen25} (dense PPL 5.18), KL distillation at 30\% sparsity achieves PPL 7.62 ($1.47\times$ dense) vs.\ TEAL vanilla PPL 8.76 ($1.69\times$), a $1.15\times$ advantage consistent with the 7--8B results (LLaMA: $1.22\times$, Mistral: $1.24\times$). % exp_id: qwen14b_kl30_s42
BCE training fails on this architecture: the predictor collapses to 6.4\% native sparsity within 50 steps, with no recovery over the remaining 950 steps. % exp_id: qwen14b_bce30_s42
A $10\times$ stronger penalty ($\lambda_{\max}{=}50{,}000$) produces identical collapse, confirming that BCE gradient insufficiency (\S\ref{sec:method:gradient}) is architecture-general. % exp_id: qwen14b_bce30_s42_v2


\paragraph{Inference Efficiency.}
The pipeline adds 6.4\% overhead to dense inference (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell) while removing the compensation network ($-$2.7\% latency, $-$67.2M params); post-hoc SPON yields $<$3\% additional gain (\S\ref{sec:spon}), suggesting the predictor already internalizes most error-correction signal (Appendix~\ref{app:latency}).

\paragraph{Training Stability.}
Three-seed evaluation (50\%): PPL $11.92 \pm 0.07$, MMLU $\sigma\leq$0.6pp (Appendix~\ref{app:stability}).
